<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
  <title>VECCOR: Transformation of VEC files of points to COR files used by
  CORRGEOM program</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
<p>This program transforms VEC files of point to the working format of CORRGEOM program. There are two options:</p>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->  

  <p>VEC input format is a standard ASCII vector file with the particularity
  that the point attribute is the final map coordinate with the values
  separated by a comma (without any blank spaces) and the point coordinate is
  the coordinate in the original reference system (not corrected).</p>

  <p>The program also allows, in the mode 2, the existence of a point label,
  that will be written after the map coordinates x,y separated by a comma
  (without any blank spaces) in the following way: x,y,label.</p>

  <p>Note: The comma ',' is always used as separator while the point
  '.' is used to indicate the decimal values.</p>

  <p>COR format has been specifically designed for the CORRGEOM program. At
  this moment, the conversion generates a simplified version that is described
  below:<br/></p>

  <ul>
    <li>COR file is an ASCII file that has in its first line the number of
    points and, in the following lines, the couple composed by the couple of
    original (non corrected) coordinates and the couple of final (map)
    coordinates, all four values being separated by spaces.</li>
  </ul>Example of input VEC file of points:<br/>
  <pre>
446000,4612000 1
1674.500000 1801.500000
447000,4613000,Tree 1
1677.000000 1378.866666
448000,4614000 1
1679.500000 853.433333
0 0
</pre>Example of output COR file:<br/>
  <pre>
3
1674.500000 1801.500000 446000 4612000 428.7
1677.000000 1378.866666 447000 4613000 541.4 Arbre
1679.500000 853.433333 448000 4614000 896.1
</pre><!-- COR output name is always the same that VEC origin file name.-->

  <p>In the option 2, where the application generates a COR file with elevations,
  the user has to set the interpolation method for obtaining elevation from
  DEM: nearest neighbour, bilinear interpolation or bicubic interpolation.</p>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
